  "checklist": [
    {
      "check_id": "CHK-0001",
      "property_id": "PROP-0042",
      "title": "Falsification Test: Can the EIP-7825 gas cap (2^24) cause the 63/64 CALL gas forwarding rule to produce incorrect forwarded gas values?",
      "severity": "Low",
      "mindset": "Formal Verification Engineer",
      "is_boundary_check": false,
      "reachability": {
        "classification": "external-reachable",
        "entry_points": [
          "Transaction"
        ],
        "attacker_controlled": true,
        "bug_bounty_scope": "in-scope"
      },
      "test_procedure": "1. Identify the two independent enforcement layers: (a) EIP-7825 gas cap enforcement in validate_transaction (transactions.py), which rejects transactions with gasLimit > TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT (16,777,216 = 2^24), and (b) the 63/64 rule in max_message_call_gas (gas.py), which computes gas - (gas // 64) on the gas_left available in the current EVM frame. 2. Verify that the gas cap is applied ONLY at transaction validation time (in validate_transaction), and does NOT modify any EVM-internal gas forwarding logic. Specifically, confirm that max_message_call_gas and calculate_message_call_gas do NOT reference TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT in any way. 3. Compute the maximum forwarded gas under the cap: with gasLimit = 16,777,216 and assuming minimal intrinsic cost (TX_BASE_COST = 21,000), the maximum available_gas entering the EVM is at most 16,777,216 - 21,000 = 16,756,216. The 63/64 rule yields floor(63/64 * 16,756,216) = floor(16,494,275.25) = 16,494,275 gas forwarded to the sub-call. Verify this value is correct and consistent across clients. 4. Verify that no integer overflow or underflow can occur in max_message_call_gas when the input is at most 16,777,216. Since gas is a Uint (arbitrary precision unsigned integer in the Python spec), overflow is impossible in the reference implementation, but check that client implementations using fixed-width integers (e.g., uint64 in Go, u64 in Rust) also handle this correctly. The value 16,777,216 fits comfortably in uint64. 5. Test with the exact boundary value: submit a transaction with gasLimit = 16,777,216, have it execute a CALL with the gas parameter set to a very large value (e.g., 2^256-1). Verify the forwarded gas is capped by the 63/64 rule to floor(63/64 * available_gas), NOT by the EIP-7825 cap. The EIP-7825 cap should be invisible to the EVM at this point. 6. Test at nested CALL depth: with gasLimit = 16,777,216, execute a chain of nested CALLs. At each depth d, the available gas decreases by factor 63/64. After 10 nested calls, available gas should be approximately 16,756,216 * (63/64)^10 which is approximately 14,178,568. Verify this value across multiple client implementations for consistency. 7. Verify that the gas parameter passed to CALL opcodes (the stack argument) is still subject to the min(requested_gas, max_message_call_gas(gas_left)) logic in calculate_message_call_gas, and that the EIP-7825 cap does not interfere with this minimum selection. 8. Check that the GAS opcode (0x5a) returns the correct gas_left value (not influenced by TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT) during execution under the cap.",
      "bug_class": "Gas Calculation Correctness / Rule Interaction",
      "risk_category": "Denial of Service",
      "code_locations": [
        {
          "file": "ethereum/forks/osaka/transactions.py",
          "function": "validate_transaction",
          "line_range": "TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT enforcement (if tx.gas > TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT: raise TransactionGasLimitExceededError)",
          "notes": "This is where the EIP-7825 cap is enforced. It is a pure validation check at transaction acceptance time, not an EVM runtime constraint."
        },
        {
          "file": "ethereum/forks/osaka/vm/gas.py",
          "function": "max_message_call_gas",
          "line_range": "return gas - (gas // Uint(64))",
          "notes": "This implements the 63/64 rule (EIP-150). It operates on gas_left in the current EVM frame, which is bounded by the transaction gasLimit but not directly coupled to TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT."
        },
        {
          "file": "ethereum/forks/osaka/vm/gas.py",
          "function": "calculate_message_call_gas",
          "line_range": "gas = min(gas, max_message_call_gas(gas_left - memory_cost - extra_gas))",
          "notes": "This applies the 63/64 rule as an upper bound on the gas forwarded to a sub-call. The requested gas from the stack is capped by this value."
        },
        {
          "file": "ethereum/forks/osaka/transactions.py",
          "constant": "TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT = Uint(16_777_216)",
          "notes": "The EIP-7825 constant. Defined alongside transaction types but NOT imported by the vm/gas.py module, confirming separation of concerns."
        }
      ],
      "executable_checks": [
        "Verify that vm/gas.py does NOT import or reference TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT anywhere, confirming the cap does not influence EVM-internal gas forwarding",
        "Unit test: calculate_message_call_gas with gas_left=16756216 (max after intrinsic cost), value=0, gas=2**256-1, memory_cost=0, extra_gas=0. Expected sub_call gas = floor(63/64 * 16756216) = 16494275",
        "Unit test: max_message_call_gas(16777216) == 16777216 - 16777216 // 64 == 16777216 - 262144 == 16515072. Verify this across geth, reth, nethermind, besu",
        "Hive test or execution-spec-tests: submit transaction with gasLimit=16777216, execute a CALL forwarding all gas, verify the sub-call receives floor(63/64 * available_gas) and not some capped value",
        "Cross-client consistency test: run the same nested CALL chain (depth=10) under gasLimit=16777216 in all major clients and verify identical gas_left values at each depth"
      ],
      "notes": "Source: PROP-0042, Source Threat: stride-016, Covers: SG-003, Edge: tb-006. This is an INVARIANT property asserting that the EIP-7825 gas cap and the 63/64 rule are orthogonal: the cap restricts what gasLimit a transaction can declare, while the 63/64 rule governs how gas is forwarded between EVM frames during execution. Analysis of the reference specification confirms these operate at different layers: TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT is checked in validate_transaction (transactions.py) while max_message_call_gas is computed in vm/gas.py with no cross-reference. The severity is Low because (a) the reference spec clearly separates these concerns, (b) the 63/64 rule predates EIP-7825 and is well-tested, and (c) the cap value 2^24 introduces no edge cases in integer arithmetic for the 63/64 computation. The primary risk is that a client implementation might incorrectly apply the gas cap inside the EVM runtime (e.g., capping forwarded gas at TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT instead of applying 63/64), which would be a consensus-level bug. Bug bounty eligible: false per the property, as the expected behavior is well-defined and the interaction is straightforward."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "CHK-0002",
      "property_id": "PROP-0042",
      "title": "Verify that no client implementation incorrectly applies TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT as a cap within EVM CALL gas forwarding logic",
      "severity": "Low",
      "mindset": "Formal Verification Engineer",
      "is_boundary_check": false,
      "reachability": {
        "classification": "external-reachable",
        "entry_points": [
          "Transaction"
        ],
        "attacker_controlled": true,
        "bug_bounty_scope": "in-scope"
      },
      "test_procedure": "1. In each major execution client, search the EVM CALL/STATICCALL/DELEGATECALL/CALLCODE opcode implementations for any reference to the EIP-7825 gas cap constant (16777216, 2^24, TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT, or equivalent). The cap should NOT appear in any EVM opcode handler. 2. For geth: search core/vm/instructions.go and core/vm/gas_table.go for references to 16777216 or params.MaxTxGasLimit. These files implement CALL gas forwarding and should only reference the 63/64 rule. 3. For reth: search crates/evm/src/interpreter/ and crates/evm/src/gas/ for references to TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT. The gas forwarding logic should only use the 63/64 computation. 4. For nethermind: search src/Nethermind/Nethermind.Evm/VirtualMachine.cs and related gas calculation files for references to the EIP-7825 constant. 5. For besu: search evm/src/main/java/org/hyperledger/besu/evm/gascalculator/ for references to the gas cap in CALL gas computations. 6. For each client, verify that the 63/64 rule implementation is: forwarded_gas = min(requested_gas, available_gas - available_gas // 64), and that this formula does NOT include any additional cap or minimum related to TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT. 7. If any client has introduced a gas cap check inside the EVM runtime (not just at transaction validation), flag this as a potential consensus bug, since the reference spec only applies the cap at validation time.",
      "bug_class": "Implementation Divergence / Consensus Bug",
      "risk_category": "Consensus Split",
      "code_locations": [
        {
          "file": "geth: core/vm/instructions.go",
          "function": "opCall / opStaticCall / opDelegateCall / opCallCode",
          "notes": "Geth CALL opcode implementations. Should use callGas() helper which implements 63/64 rule without referencing the transaction gas cap."
        },
        {
          "file": "geth: core/vm/gas_table.go",
          "function": "gasCall / gasCallCode / gasDelegateCall / gasStaticCall",
          "notes": "Geth gas calculation for CALL opcodes. Should implement 63/64 rule only."
        },
        {
          "file": "reth: crates/interpreter/src/instructions/host.rs",
          "function": "call / static_call / delegate_call / call_code",
          "notes": "Reth CALL opcode implementations."
        },
        {
          "file": "nethermind: src/Nethermind/Nethermind.Evm/VirtualMachine.cs",
          "function": "CALL handling section",
          "notes": "Nethermind EVM CALL implementation."
        },
        {
          "file": "besu: evm/src/main/java/org/hyperledger/besu/evm/operation/CallOperation.java",
          "function": "execute / gasCalculator.callOperationGasCost",
          "notes": "Besu CALL operation implementation."
        }
      ],
      "executable_checks": [
        "Static analysis: grep for TX_MAX_GAS_LIMIT / MaxTxGasLimit / 16777216 / 0x1000000 in EVM opcode handler files across all clients. Should return zero matches in EVM runtime code.",
        "Differential test: execute identical CALL-forwarding contracts across geth, reth, nethermind, besu with gasLimit=16777216 and verify identical gas_left traces at every CALL depth",
        "Property-based test: for all valid gasLimit values g in [21000, 16777216], for all requested CALL gas values r, the forwarded gas equals min(r, floor(63/64 * (g - intrinsic_cost - executed_gas)))"
      ],
      "notes": "Source: PROP-0042. This check complements CHK-0001 by focusing on the implementation layer rather than the specification layer. While the reference spec clearly separates the gas cap from the 63/64 rule, individual client implementations could introduce coupling between these two mechanisms. The most likely error would be a client developer adding a redundant cap check inside the EVM CALL handler, thinking it provides defense-in-depth, but actually changing the gas forwarding semantics and causing a consensus divergence from clients that correctly implement the spec. Since the EIP-7825 cap (2^24 = 16,777,216) is a new constant introduced in Fusaka, all clients needed to add it to their codebase. The risk is that a developer places this constant in the wrong location (EVM runtime) in addition to or instead of the correct location (transaction validation)."
    }
  ],
